body:
### Feature request description

Podman 5.8 introduced podman exec --no-session, which avoids persistent exec-session tracking and invokes the OCI runtime without an exec-session conmon. This is currently available only through local Podman; the remote implementation returns:

--no-session is not supported for the remote client

I would like an equivalent synchronous operation exposed through the Libpod REST API.

This is related to a production scalability problem with high-rate remote exec workloads. Every completed API exec retains conmon processes for exit_command_delay. When the delay expires, each retained conmon launches a standalone command resembling:

podman ... container cleanup --stopped-only --rm --exec <exec-id> <container-id>

At sufficient exec volume, retained conmons consume substantial memory and PIDs, followed by a large concurrent wave of Podman cleanup CLI processes.

In a controlled 512-concurrency load test, this caused the Podman runtime cgroup to reach its exact 80 GiB limit and be OOM-killed. Immediately before the failure:

runtime-side process count had grown from approximately 70 to 35,088;
the runtime daemon cgroup reached 52.1 GiB;
48.1 GiB was anonymous memory;
the sandbox payload cgroup was only 27.9 GiB;
the pod and node had not reached their memory limits.

An isolated test using one existing container and 400 concurrent true execs reproduced the amplification without concurrent container creation or deletion:

all 400 execs succeeded in 14.3 seconds;
conmon grew from 1 to 685 processes;
daemon PIDs grew from 71 to 800;
daemon-cgroup memory grew from 6.82 GiB to 11.26 GiB;
summed Podman RSS grew from 8.73 GiB to 14.95 GiB.

After delayed cleanup completed, process and memory counts returned toward baseline.

### Suggest potential solution

Add a Podman-specific synchronous Libpod endpoint, conceptually:

POST /libpod/containers/{name}/exec-no-session

The endpoint should:

- call the existing local sessionless-exec implementation;
- avoid creating or persisting an exec-session record;
- avoid starting conmon for the exec;
- support stdin, stdout and stderr streaming;
- return the final exit code through the same request;
- propagate cancellation or client disconnection to the running process;
- not support detached execution.

This does not need to be part of the Docker-compatible API. The Docker exec protocol assumes a persistent exec ID followed by separate start and inspect operations, whereas sessionless exec is naturally a single synchronous request.

The exact response framing is open for discussion. One option would be Podman-specific multiplexed framing with a final exit-status control frame.

I am willing to implement and contribute this endpoint if the maintainers agree with the API direction.

### Have you considered any alternatives?

Yes:

- Reduce exit_command_delay: Tested with a reduction from 300 seconds to 10 seconds. This moved the cleanup wave into peak load and caused the runtime to OOM after approximately 168 seconds, substantially earlier than with the default delay.
- Explicitly remove each completed exec: Tested using POST /v4.0.0/libpod/exec/{id}/remove. Podman removed the stopped exec record and bundle, but the already-sleeping conmon processes remained and still retained their delayed cleanup commands.
- Increase the runtime memory limit: This would delay the failure but would not bound retained process count or cleanup concurrency.
- Invoke local podman exec --no-session through a separate helper service: This is viable, but it requires an additional local component solely because podman system service does not expose the existing sessionless path.
- Invoke crun exec directly: This avoids the retained-session mechanism but requires the caller to reproduce Podman’s user, environment, working-directory, TTY, streaming, exit-code, cancellation and container-lifecycle behavior.

### Additional context

The retained remote-exec conmon behavior has previously been reported upstream. The earlier report observed two additional conmon processes per remote exec and no corresponding accumulation for local exec.

The original sessionless-exec request explicitly scoped the feature to local Podman. This request is specifically for a new synchronous Libpod API operation that provides equivalent behavior to applications using podman system service.

A successful implementation should be validated with repeated exec-only bursts and should demonstrate:

- no retained exec conmons after completion;
- no delayed standalone Podman cleanup wave;
- memory and PID counts returning toward baseline;
- correct behavior during cancellation, timeout, container deletion and Podman service restart.
